  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The growth rate of the rabbit population $P(t)$ in a wildlife preserve is jointly proportional to the rabbit population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the preserve’s carrying capacity and the rabbit population.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.0025$ and the carrying capacity is $1200,$ find an expression for the rabbit population after $t$ years, given that there were $200$ rabbits in the preserve initially.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n  <tbody><tr>\n    <td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"right\">\n      $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$\n    </td>\n    <td align=\"center\">\n      $1200$\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td align=\"center\">\n      $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}}\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "Since the rabbit population’s growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the rabbit population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the rabbit population, the rabbit population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0025,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1200.$ Therefore, the rabbit population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0025\\,P(1200-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-(1200\\cdot 0.0025)t}}=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=200$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}} & =200 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1200}{200} \\\\ C & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$",
